## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-repo data sharing and multi-region provider patterns, plus a practical gating approach to prevent drift.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across regions
- data.terraform_remote_state for read-only cross-repo data
- Per-region backends and workflows
- CI policy gates to enforce drift checks and approvals
- Cross-region resource referencing of VPC/subnet IDs

## Code Example
```javascript
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.3"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "east"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
data "terraform_remote_state" "net" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "infra-network-bucket"
    key    = "network/us-east-1/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
# usage
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  provider    = aws.west
  ami         = "ami-0abcdef1234567890"
  subnet_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.net.outputs.public_subnet_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across repos? 
- How would you handle regional failover and state restoration?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical understanding of per-env isolation using workspaces, ensuring policy via gates, not just code.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform workspaces
- GCS backend prefixing
- Wrapper modules for consistent labeling
- GitHub Actions plan gating with json + jq

## Code Example
```javascript
// wrappers/env_wrap/main.tf (skeleton)
variable 'environment' {}
variable 'cost_center' {}
```
```javascript
// backend.tf (skeleton)
terraform {
  backend 'gcs' {
    bucket = tf_state_bucket
    prefix = terraform/${terraform_workspace}
  }
}
```
```javascript
// gate.sh (skeleton)
terraform init
terraform plan -out=tfplan
PLAN_JSON=$(terraform show -json tfplan)
# simple policy: if changes exist beyond tag updates, fail
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce per-environment IAM changes safely?
- How would you extend the gate to support selective resource types?
